  "steps": [
    {
      "id": "c1",
      "kind": "concept",
      "body": "When substitution gives **0/0**, that's the **indeterminate form** — it doesn't mean the limit is 0 or undefined; it means 'more work needed.' The fix for rational functions: **factor** the numerator and denominator, **cancel** the shared factor causing the 0/0, then substitute into what remains.",
      "figure": "lc-indeterminate"
    },
    {
      "id": "i1",
      "kind": "interactive",
      "body": "The 0/0 form, magnified.",
      "widget": {
        "type": "graphZoom",
        "behaviour": "removable",
        "a": 2,
        "leftValue": 4,
        "rightValue": 4,
        "fAtA": null,
        "targetVerdict": "limit-exists",
        "requiredZoom": 3,
        "prompt": "f(x) = (x² − 4)/(x − 2) gives 0/0 at x = 2. Magnify there and decide whether the limit exists.",
        "successFeedback": "Cancelling (x − 2) leaves x + 2, which is 4 at x = 2 — and the graph agrees: both sides go to 4 while the hole stays open. Cancelling is not cheating, because a limit never asks about x = 2 itself, only about x NEAR 2.",
        "moreZoomFeedback": "Magnify further. 0/0 is not an answer, it is a signal that the point itself is telling you nothing — so go and look at its neighbourhood.",
        "wrongVerdictFeedback": "0/0 means f(2) is undefined; it does not mean the limit fails. Watch the readouts: both sides settle on 4 and stay there."
      },
      "predict": {
        "prompt": "You'll zoom in at x = 2. What will the two sides of the graph do?",
        "options": [
          {
            "id": "agree",
            "label": "Close in on the same height"
