    {
      "id": "k1",
      "kind": "check",
      "body": "Factor and cancel.",
      "conceptTag": "lc-factor",
      "explanationVariants": [
        "x² − 5x + 6 = (x − 2)(x − 3), so (x² − 5x + 6)/(x − 2) = x − 3 for x ≠ 2, giving lim = 2 − 3 = −1.",
        "Cancel (x − 2): left with x − 3 → −1."
      ],
      "widget": {
        "type": "numeric",
        "prompt": "Find lim(x→2) (x² − 5x + 6)/(x − 2). (Factor: (x − 2)(x − 3).)",
        "answer": -1,
        "tolerance": 0,
        "commonErrors": [
          {
            "value": 0,
            "feedback": "0/0 is indeterminate, not 0. Cancel to x − 3 → −1."
          },
          {
            "value": 1,
            "feedback": "After cancelling: x − 3 = 2 − 3 = −1, not 1."
          }
        ],
        "fallbackFeedback": "(x − 2)(x − 3)/(x − 2) = x − 3 → −1."
      },
      "variant": {
        "gen": "g12-limits-continuity",
        "form": "limits-continuity__lc-factor__numeric"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "c2",
      "kind": "concept",
      "body": "Why cancelling is legal: for a limit, x approaches 2 but never EQUALS 2, so x − 2 ≠ 0 the whole time and dividing it out is valid. The cancelled expression matches the original everywhere near 2 — exactly where the limit lives. The removed factor was just the hole.",
      "figure": "lc-cancel-legal"
    },
